[{"id":"fm-core2-2.45-q1","number":1,"question":"<p>The price <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p(t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, in pound sterling, of a particular type of fresh strawberries sold by a large chain of supermarkets is modelled over time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> days after a sudden oversupply. Due to delays in adjusting pricing across stores and responding to customer demand, the price is assumed to satisfy</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n4\\frac{d^2p}{dt^2}+4\\frac{dp}{dt}+p=0.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>At time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the price is £10 per kg and is decreasing at a rate of £2 per day.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the general solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find the particular solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Determine whether the price, according to the model, ever becomes negative.</p></li><li><p>Find the time at which the rate of decrease of the price is greatest.</p></li><li><p>Explain one limitation of this model in the context of supermarket pricing.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>Auxiliary equation:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"4m^2+4m+1=0\\implies(2m+1)^2=0\\implies m=-\\tfrac12.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Hence the general solution is</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"p=At e^{-t/2}+Be^{-t/2}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>Substitute <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p=10\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"10=A\\times0\\times e^0+Be^0\\implies B=10.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p=At e^{-t/2}+10e^{-t/2}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Differentiating,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\frac{dp}{dt}=-\\tfrac12At e^{-t/2}+Ae^{-t/2}-5e^{-t/2}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Substitute <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p'=-2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"-2=-\\tfrac12A\\times0\\times e^0+Ae^0-5e^0\\implies -2=A-5\\implies A=3.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Therefore</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"p=3t e^{-t/2}+10e^{-t/2}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>For all <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\geq0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^{-t/2}&gt;0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3t e^{-t/2}+10e^{-t/2}&gt;0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> for all <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\geq0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so the modelled price never becomes negative.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>From <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p=3t e^{-t/2}+10e^{-t/2}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"p'=-\\tfrac32t e^{-t/2}-2e^{-t/2},\\qquad p''=\\tfrac34t e^{-t/2}-\\tfrac12e^{-t/2}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>The rate of decrease is greatest when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p''=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"(3t-2)e^{-t/2}=0\\implies t=\\tfrac23.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>So the rate of decrease is greatest when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=\\tfrac23\" data-display=\"false\"></span> days.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p>One limitation is that the model assumes the price changes continuously and according to fixed coefficients throughout the time period. It does not consider promotions, competitor prices, stock levels, supplier decisions, customer demand, etc.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.45-q2","number":2,"question":"<p>A heavy fire door is released and begins to close automatically. Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta(t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> be the angular displacement, in radians, of the door from the fully closed position at time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> seconds. The motion is modelled by</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac{d^2\\theta}{dt^2}+5\\frac{d\\theta}{dt}+6\\theta=0.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>Initially, the door is held open at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta=0.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and released from rest.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the general solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find the particular solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find the time at which the angular speed of the door is greatest.</p></li><li><p>Find the angular velocity when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Comment on the suitability of this model for large values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>The auxiliary equation is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m^2+5m+6=0=(m+2)(m+3)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m=-2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> or <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m=-3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta=Ae^{-2t}+Be^{-3t}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>Substitute <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta=0.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"0.5=Ae^0+Be^0\\implies A+B=0.5.\\tag{1}\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Also <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta'=-2Ae^{-2t}-3Be^{-3t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Substitute <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta'=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"0=-2Ae^0-3Be^0\\implies 2A+3B=0.\\tag{2}\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Solving (1) and (2) gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=\\tfrac32\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B=-1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, hence</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta=\\tfrac32e^{-2t}-e^{-3t}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta'=-3e^{-2t}+3e^{-3t},\\qquad \\theta''=6e^{-2t}-9e^{-3t}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>The angular speed is greatest when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta''=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"6e^t-9=0\\implies e^t=\\tfrac32\\implies t=\\ln\\tfrac32=0.405\\text{ s (3 SF)}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\left.\\frac{d\\theta}{dt}\\right|_{t=1}=-3e^{-2}+3e^{-3}=(-3e^{-2}+3e^{-3})\\text{ rad s}^{-1}=-0.257\\text{ rad s}^{-1}\\text{ (3 SF)}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^{-2t}\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^{-3t}\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta(t)\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The model predicts that the door approaches the closed position, which is reasonable. However, a real door will come to rest exactly at the closed position, and stop suddenly, rather than follow a perfectly smooth exponential model for all time.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.45-q3","number":3,"question":"<p>A carriage on a rotating unfair ride is displaced slightly from its equilibrium position. Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta(t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> be the angular displacement, in radians, from equilibrium at time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> seconds. The motion is modelled by</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n9\\frac{d^2\\theta}{dt^2}+6\\frac{d\\theta}{dt}+2\\theta=0.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>At time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and the carriage is given an initial angular velocity of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3\\text{ rad s}^{-1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the general solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find the particular solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Explain why the motion is oscillatory.</p></li><li><p>Find the maximum angular displacement after <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Find the second time at which the carriage comes instantaneously to rest.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>The auxiliary equation is</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"9m^2+6m+2=0\\implies m^2+\\tfrac23m+\\tfrac29=0\\implies(m+\\tfrac13)^2=-\\tfrac19,\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m=-\\tfrac13\\pm\\tfrac13i\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta=e^{-t/3}\\left(A\\cos\\tfrac t3+B\\sin\\tfrac t3\\right).\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>Using <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta(0)=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta=Be^{-t/3}\\sin(t/3)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Differentiating,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta'=\\tfrac13Be^{-t/3}\\cos(t/3)-\\tfrac13Be^{-t/3}\\sin(t/3).\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Since <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta'(0)=3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3=\\tfrac13B\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, hence <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B=9\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Therefore</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta=9e^{-t/3}\\sin\\tfrac t3.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>The particular solution contains <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin(t/3)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, which changes sign repeatedly as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> increases. Therefore <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta(t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> alternates between positive and negative values and the carriage moves repeatedly from one side of equilibrium to the other; the motion is oscillatory. The factor <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^{-t/3}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> means the oscillations decrease in size with time.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta'=3e^{-t/3}\\cos(t/3)-3e^{-t/3}\\sin(t/3).\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>A maximum occurs when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta'=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\tan(t/3)=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=3\\arctan 1=3\\pi/4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Then</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta\\big|_{t=3\\pi/4}=9e^{-\\pi/4}\\sin(\\pi/4)=\\frac{9\\sqrt2}{2}e^{-\\pi/4}=2.90\\text{ rad (3 SF)}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p>The carriage is instantaneously at rest when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta'=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. From part (d), <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\tan(t/3)=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, with period <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi/(1/3)=3\\pi\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The second positive time is</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"t=3\\arctan1+3\\pi=\\frac{15\\pi}{4}=11.8\\text{ s (3 SF)}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.45-q4","number":4,"question":"<p>A water tank is part of a control system that regulates water level. Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h(t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, in metres, be the deviation of the water level from its equilibrium level at time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> seconds. The system is modelled by</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac{d^2h}{dt^2}+\\frac{dh}{dt}+\\frac{2}{9}h=0.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>At time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the water level is 0.3 m above equilibrium and is falling at a rate of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0.3\\text{ m s}^{-1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the general solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find the particular solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find the time at which the water level first reaches equilibrium.</p></li><li><p>Find the greatest deviation of the water level below equilibrium, and the time at which this occurs.</p></li><li><p>Find the rate of change of water level at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>The auxiliary equation is</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"m^2+m+\\tfrac29=0\\implies(m+\\tfrac12)^2=\\tfrac1{36}\\implies m=-\\tfrac13\\text{ or }-\\tfrac23.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Hence <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=Ae^{-t/3}+Be^{-2t/3}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>From <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h(0)=0.3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A+B=\\tfrac3{10}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Also <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h'=-\\tfrac13Ae^{-t/3}-\\tfrac23Be^{-2t/3}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h'(0)=-0.3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-\\tfrac13A-\\tfrac23B=-\\tfrac3{10}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Solving gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=-\\tfrac3{10}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B=\\tfrac35\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"h=-\\tfrac3{10}e^{-t/3}+\\tfrac35e^{-2t/3}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>At equilibrium <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"-\\tfrac3{10}e^{-t/3}+\\tfrac35e^{-2t/3}=0\\implies-e^{-t/3}+2e^{-2t/3}=0\\implies-e^{t/3}+2=0.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^{t/3}=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=3\\ln2=2.08\\text{ s (3 SF)}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"h'=\\tfrac1{10}e^{-t/3}-\\tfrac25e^{-2t/3}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>The greatest deviation below equilibrium occurs when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h'=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\tfrac1{10}e^{-t/3}-\\tfrac25e^{-2t/3}=0\\implies e^{-t/3}-4e^{-2t/3}=0\\implies e^{t/3}-4=0.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^{t/3}=4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=3\\ln4=4.16\\text{ (3 SF)}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. At this time,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\nh =-\\tfrac3{10}e^{-\\frac13(3\\ln4)}+\\tfrac35e^{-\\frac23(3\\ln4)}\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n =-\\tfrac3{10}e^{-\\ln4}+\\tfrac35e^{-2\\ln4}\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n =-\\tfrac3{10}e^{\\ln(4^{-1})}+\\tfrac35e^{\\ln(4^{-2})}\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n =-\\tfrac3{10}\\times\\tfrac14+\\tfrac35\\times\\tfrac1{16}=-\\tfrac3{80}\\text{ m}=-0.0375\\text{ m}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>So the greatest deviation below equilibrium is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0.0375\" data-display=\"false\"></span> m, at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=4.16\" data-display=\"false\"></span> s.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\left.\\frac{dh}{dt}\\right|_{t=3}=\\tfrac1{10}e^{-1}-\\tfrac25e^{-2}=-0.0173\\text{ m s}^{-1}\\text{ (3 SF)}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.45-q5","number":5,"question":"<p>A camera on a stabilising tripod is displaced from its equilibrium position, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> m is the horizontal displacement from equilibrium at time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> s. The motion is modelled by</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac{d^2x}{dt^2}+a\\frac{dx}{dt}+bx=0,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are positive constants. At time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, we have that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=-0.02\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dx}{dt}=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{d^2x}{dt^2}=8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. It is given that the auxiliary equation has repeated roots.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Find the particular solution.</p></li><li><p>Describe the motion of the camera as time increases.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>Substituting <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=-0.02\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x'=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x''=8\" data-display=\"false\"></span> into <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x''+ax'+bx=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"8-0.02b=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b=400\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The auxiliary equation is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m^2+am+400=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Repeated roots mean <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a^2-4(1)(400)=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a^2=1600\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Since <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a&gt;0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=40\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Therefore <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=40\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b=400\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>The auxiliary equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m^2+40m+400=0=(m+20)^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=At e^{-20t}+Be^{-20t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. From <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x(0)=-0.02\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B=-0.02=-\\tfrac1{50}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Differentiating,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"x'=-20At e^{-20t}+Ae^{-20t}+\\tfrac25e^{-20t}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Using <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x'(0)=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=-\\tfrac25\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"x=-\\tfrac25t e^{-20t}-\\tfrac1{50}e^{-20t}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^{-20t}\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"te^{-t}\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The displacement never becomes positive, so the camera does not pass through equilibrium and does not oscillate. It approaches equilibrium as time increases.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.45-q6","number":6,"question":"<p>A car is travelling along a straight road. Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"v(t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, in <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\text{m s}^{-1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, be its speed at time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> seconds. The motion is modelled by</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac{d^2v}{dt^2}+\\frac32\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac12v=4.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>Initially, the car has speed <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0\\text{ m s}^{-1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and acceleration <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1\\text{ m s}^{-2}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the general solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find the particular solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find the time at which the acceleration is greatest.</p></li><li><p>Find the limiting speed of the car.</p></li><li><p>Comment on the suitability of this model for large values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>For the homogeneous equation the auxiliary equation is</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"m^2+\\tfrac32m+\\tfrac12=0\\implies(m+\\tfrac34)^2=\\tfrac1{16}\\implies m=-\\tfrac12\\text{ or }-1.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Thus the complementary function is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"v=Ae^{-t/2}+Be^{-t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Try a constant particular integral <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"v=k\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Substitution gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\tfrac12k=4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"v=Ae^{-t/2}+Be^{-t}+8.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>From <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"v(0)=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A+B=-8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Also <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"v'=-\\tfrac12Ae^{-t/2}-Be^{-t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"v'(0)=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-\\tfrac12A-B=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Solving gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=-14\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B=6\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"v=-14e^{-t/2}+6e^{-t}+8.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"v'=7e^{-t/2}-6e^{-t},\\qquad v''=-\\tfrac72e^{-t/2}+6e^{-t}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>The greatest acceleration occurs when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"v''=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"-\\tfrac72e^{-t/2}+6e^{-t}=0\\implies e^{t/2}=\\tfrac{12}{7}\\implies t=2\\ln\\tfrac{12}{7}=1.08\\text{ s (3 SF)}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, both exponential terms tend to zero, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"v\\to8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The limiting speed is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"8\\text{ m s}^{-1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p>The model predicts that the speed approaches a constant limiting value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"8\\text{ m s}^{-1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. A real car would not usually continue under exactly the same conditions. In practice, the motion would be affected by factors such as changes in driver input, road conditions, gear changes, varying resistance, running out of petrol, and the inability to travel in a straight line indefinitely due to terrain.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.45-q7","number":7,"question":"<p>A room is being heated by a system whose output increases steadily over time. Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T(t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, in <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^\\circ\\text{C}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, be the temperature of the room at time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> minutes. The temperature is modelled by</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac{d^2T}{dt^2}+3\\frac{dT}{dt}+2T=17+t.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>At time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the temperature is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"8^\\circ\\text{C}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and is increasing at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0.25^\\circ\\text{C}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> per minute.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the general solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find the particular solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Show that the temperature is always increasing.</p></li><li><p>Find the temperature as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Comment on the suitability of this model.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>The homogeneous auxiliary equation is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m^2+3m+2=0=(m+1)(m+2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so the complementary function is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"v=Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Try <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T=pt+q\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T'=p\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T''=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\frac{d^2T}{dt^2}+3\\frac{dT}{dt}+2T=12+t.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac{d^2T}{dt^2}+3\\frac{dT}{dt}+2T =17+t,\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n(0)+3(p)+2(pt+q) =17+t,\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n(2p)t+(3p+2q) =t+17.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Comparing coefficients gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2p=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p=\\tfrac12\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3\\times\\tfrac12+2q=17\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q=\\tfrac{31}{4}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The particular integral is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T=\\tfrac12t+\\tfrac{31}{4}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"T=Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}+\\tfrac12t+\\tfrac{31}{4}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>Using <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T(0)=8\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A+B=\\tfrac14\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Also <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T'=-Ae^{-t}-2Be^{-2t}+\\tfrac12\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T'(0)=0.25\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A+2B=\\tfrac14\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=\\tfrac14\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, and</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"T=\\tfrac14e^{-t}+\\tfrac12t+\\tfrac{31}{4}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"T'=-\\tfrac14e^{-t}+\\tfrac12.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>The temperature is increasing when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T'&gt;0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, which is equivalent to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^t&gt;\\tfrac12\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Since <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\geq0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^t\\geq1&gt;\\tfrac12\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so the temperature is always increasing.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the exponential term vanishes and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T\\sim\\tfrac12t+\\tfrac{31}{4}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p>The model may be reasonable over a short period if the heating output is steadily increasing, but it would not be realistic for a long time. The heater cannot increase output indefinitely, and the room temperature would not continue rising without limit.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.45-q8","number":8,"question":"<p>A tank is being filled by a pump whose output increases over time, while water simultaneously drains from the tank. Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h(t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, in metres, be the height of water above a reference level at time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> seconds. The system is modelled by</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac{d^2h}{dt^2}+\\frac{dh}{dt}+\\frac14h=t^2+2.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>At time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, it is given that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dh}{dt}=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the general solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find the particular solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h''(0)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and interpret what this means in context.</p></li><li><p>Determine whether, over time, the water level increases without bound.</p></li><li><p>Explain why this model may not be realistic for large values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>The homogeneous auxiliary equation is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m^2+m+\\tfrac14=0=(m+\\tfrac12)^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving complementary function <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=At e^{-t/2}+Be^{-t/2}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Try <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=pt^2+qt+r\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h'=2pt+q\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h''=2p\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Substitution gives</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"2p+(2pt+q)+\\tfrac14(pt^2+qt+r)=t^2+2.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Comparing coefficients: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p/4=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p=4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>; <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2p+q/4=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q=-32\" data-display=\"false\"></span>; and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2p+q+r/4=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r=104\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Therefore</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"h=At e^{-t/2}+Be^{-t/2}+4t^2-32t+104.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>From <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h(0)=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B=-104\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Differentiating,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"h'=-\\tfrac12At e^{-t/2}+Ae^{-t/2}+52e^{-t/2}+8t-32.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Using <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h'(0)=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A+52-32=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=-20\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Therefore</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"h=-20t e^{-t/2}-104e^{-t/2}+4t^2-32t+104.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"h'=10t e^{-t/2}+32e^{-t/2}+8t-32,\\qquad h''=-5t e^{-t/2}-6e^{-t/2}+8.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h''(0)=-6+8=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the rate of increase of the water level is increasing at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\\text{ m s}^{-2}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^{-t/2}\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"te^{-t/2}\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h\\sim4t^2-32t+104\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The dominant term is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4t^2\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so the water level increases without bound.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p>The model predicts that the water level increases without bound as time increases. This is not realistic for a real tank as the tank would have a finite capacity, and the pump output could not continue increasing indefinitely. The model may be useful over a short period of time, but it would not remain valid for large values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.45-q9","number":9,"question":"<p>A rotating platform at a fairground is subjected to a periodic driving force from its motor. Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta(t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, in radians, be the angular displacement from equilibrium at time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> seconds. The motion is modelled by</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n80\\frac{d^2\\theta}{dt^2}+40\\frac{d\\theta}{dt}+25\\theta=136\\cos\\frac12t.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>At time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the platform is displaced by 0.4 radians from equilibrium and is rotating back towards equilibrium with angular velocity <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0.5\\text{ rad s}^{-1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta\" data-display=\"false\"></span> in terms of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Describe the long-term behaviour of the motion.</p></li><li><p>Write the steady-state motion in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"R\\cos\\left(\\frac12t-\\alpha\\right)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"R\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\alpha\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are constants.</p></li><li><p>The platform is regarded as safe only if its steady-state angular displacement never exceeds 6.5 radians in magnitude. Determine whether the platform is safe.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>The homogeneous auxiliary equation is</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"80m^2+40m+25=0\\implies m^2+\\tfrac12m+\\tfrac5{16}=0\\implies(m+\\tfrac14)^2=-\\tfrac14,\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m=-\\tfrac14\\pm\\tfrac12i\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The complementary function is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta=e^{-t/4}(A\\cos(t/2)+B\\sin(t/2))\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Try <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta=p\\cos(t/2)+q\\sin(t/2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Then</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta'=-\\tfrac12p\\sin(t/2)+\\tfrac12q\\cos(t/2),\\quad \\theta''=-\\tfrac14p\\cos(t/2)-\\tfrac14q\\sin(t/2).\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Substitution gives</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"(5p+20q)\\cos(t/2)+(-20p+5q)\\sin(t/2)=136\\cos(t/2),\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"5p+20q=136\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-20p+5q=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p=\\tfrac85\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q=\\tfrac{32}{5}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving the general solution</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta=e^{-t/4}(A\\cos(t/2)+B\\sin(t/2))+\\tfrac85\\cos(t/2)+\\tfrac{32}{5}\\sin(t/2).\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Using <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta(0)=0.4\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=-\\tfrac65\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Differentiating the general solution and using <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta'(0)=-0.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B=-8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Therefore</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta=e^{-t/4}(-\\tfrac65\\cos(t/2)-8\\sin(t/2))+\\tfrac85\\cos(t/2)+\\tfrac{32}{5}\\sin(t/2).\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^{-t/4}\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so for large <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span>,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\theta\\sim\\tfrac85\\cos(t/2)+\\tfrac{32}{5}\\sin(t/2).\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>The platform approaches a steady oscillation with period <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\\pi/(1/2)=4\\pi\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>Write <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\tfrac85\\cos(t/2)+\\tfrac{32}{5}\\sin(t/2)\\equiv R\\cos(t/2-\\alpha)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Comparing coefficients gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"R\\cos\\alpha=\\tfrac85\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"R\\sin\\alpha=\\tfrac{32}{5}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"R=\\sqrt{(\\tfrac85)^2+(\\tfrac{32}{5})^2}=\\tfrac{8\\sqrt{17}}5,\\qquad \\tan\\alpha=4,\\quad\\alpha=\\arctan4=1.33\\text{ (3 SF)}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Therefore the steady-state motion is</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\frac{8\\sqrt{17}}5\\cos\\left(\\tfrac12t-\\arctan4\\right).\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"R=\\frac{8\\sqrt{17}}5=6.596969\\ldots&gt;6.5.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>The steady-state amplitude exceeds <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"6.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so the platform cannot be regarded as safe.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.45-q10","number":10,"question":"<p>A chemical reactor is heated by an external process. The temperature <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T(t)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, measured in <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^\\circ\\text{C}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> above a reference level, changes over time as heat is supplied to the reactor. A model for the temperature is</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n36\\frac{d^2T}{dt^2}+108\\frac{dT}{dt}+81T=216e^{-\\frac32t}+100e^{\\frac16t},\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is measured in minutes. Initially, the temperature is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4^\\circ\\text{C}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> above the reference level and is decreasing at a rate of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac13\\, ^\\circ\\text{C min}^{-1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the general solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Find the particular solution of the differential equation.</p></li><li><p>Describe the behaviour of the temperature as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Estimate the temperature in the reactor after 10 minutes to 3 significant figures.</p></li><li><p>Estimate the time at which the temperature in the reactor is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"20^\\circ\\text{C}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>The homogeneous auxiliary equation is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"36m^2+108m+81=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, or <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(m+\\tfrac32)^2=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The complementary function is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T=At e^{-3t/2}+Be^{-3t/2}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Try <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T=pt^2e^{-3t/2}+qe^{t/6}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Then</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\nT' =-\\tfrac32pt^2e^{-3t/2}+2pt e^{-3t/2}+\\tfrac16qe^{t/6},\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\nT'' =\\tfrac94pt^2e^{-3t/2}-6pt e^{-3t/2}+2pe^{-3t/2}+\\tfrac1{36}qe^{t/6}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Substitution into <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"36T''+108T'+81T=216e^{-3t/2}+100e^{t/6}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"(72p)e^{-3t/2}+(q+18q+81q)e^{t/6}=216e^{-3t/2}+100e^{t/6}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"72p=216\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"100q=100\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p=3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The general solution is</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"T=At e^{-3t/2}+Be^{-3t/2}+3t^2e^{-3t/2}+e^{t/6}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>Using <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T(0)=4\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B=3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Then</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"T'= -\\tfrac32At e^{-3t/2}+Ae^{-3t/2}-\\tfrac92e^{-3t/2}-\\tfrac92t^2e^{-3t/2}+6te^{-3t/2}+\\tfrac16e^{t/6}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Using <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T'(0)=-\\tfrac13\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-\\tfrac13=A-\\tfrac92+\\tfrac16\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"T=4t e^{-3t/2}+3e^{-3t/2}+3t^2e^{-3t/2}+e^{t/6}=(3t^2+4t+3)e^{-3t/2}+e^{t/6}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(3t^2+4t+3)e^{-3t/2}\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> because the exponential tends to zero faster than the quadratic increases, while <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^{t/6}\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T\\sim e^{t/6}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and the temperature increases without bound.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"T(10)=(3\\times10^2+4\\times10+3)e^{-3(10)/2}+e^{10/6}=0.0001049\\ldots+5.29449\\ldots=5.29^\\circ\\text{C (3 SF)}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p>Set <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"20=(3t^2+4t+3)e^{-3t/2}+e^{t/6}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. By the time the temperature is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"20^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(3t^2+4t+3)e^{-3t/2}\\approx0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Estimate by solving</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"20=e^{t/6}\\implies t=6\\ln20=17.97439\\ldots=18.0\\text{ minutes (3 SF)}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]}]